 HOW TO READ THIS TABLE, and the last row is what makes it
 readable. binCV has NO null-stream guard anywhere, so its row is
 the control: whatever ratio IT shows is the legacy default stream's
 own per-launch tax, which every arm on that stream pays -- the
 legacy stream implicitly synchronizes with every other blocking
 stream in the process, and one exists here. Only the EXCESS over
 the control is attributable to OpenCV's cudaDeviceSynchronize.

 AND THE SIZE OF THE EFFECT DEPENDS ON THE KERNEL, which is the
 conclusion that actually matters for this report: a synchronize
 costs roughly one launch latency, so it is most of the measurement
 for an op sitting near the launch floor and almost none of it for
 an op that runs for a millisecond. Expect threshold, resize and
 median to move a lot and StereoBM to barely move at all -- and
 check that against the rows above rather than taking it on trust.

