=====================================================================
 17. OUTSTANDING (ruling R2) -- round 2's ops with NO cv::cuda
     counterpart at any API level
=====================================================================
 Each of these ships on correctness, memory and the HOST comparison,
 with its SPEED verdict recorded OUTSTANDING. No substitute bar is
 invented and no CPU number is quoted as a GPU one.

 gradientCovarianceAsync / gradientCovarianceBatchAsync
     Neither cv::cuda nor cv:: computes a 2x2 gradient covariance at
     any API level. cornerHarris and createMinEigenValCorner compute
     a dense float RESPONSE THROUGH one -- that is the bar for a
     composed corner op, which section 11 runs, and it is not a bar
     for the covariance itself.

 cornerSubPixAsync
     No cv::cuda counterpart in any module. Decided instead by its
     own round-trip inequality, which its author reports it MISSES.

 goodFeaturesToTrack's DEVICE-RESIDENT SPACING
     Section 10 times the whole op against OpenCV's. The residency
     itself -- a greedy min-distance filter that never leaves the
     device -- has no counterpart to be timed against, because
     OpenCV's runs on the host by construction.

 keypointsFromCorners
     Tier 3. OpenCV does not have the problem: its detectors already
     hand back float2, so there is nothing to convert and nothing to
     compare.

 keypointOrientation (all three arms)
     cv::cuda::ORB runs IC_Angle inside its keypoint pass and exposes
     no entry point that orients provided keypoints. Section 12 says
     so at the number.

 derivativeX / derivativeY / derivativeXY over TERNARY planes
     Section 11's inputs. cv::cuda::createDerivFilter is the nearest
     denominator and the derivcov family times it; both arms sit ON
     the launch floor there, so that ratio is a lower bound on the
     gap and says nothing about binCV's kernel.

 ROUND 3'S ADDITIONS TO THIS LIST.

 stereoDescriptorMatch / stereoRefineDisparity /
 stereoMatchRectified
     Sparse rectified stereo by descriptor match and a one-bit
     window refinement. cv::cuda ships dense block matchers
     (StereoBM, StereoBeliefPropagation, StereoConstantSpaceBP)
     and no SPARSE stereo at any API level -- a dense map for
     500 keypoints is a different operation, not a slower
     spelling of this one. Priced instead against binCV's OWN
     device dense path, which is a binCV-to-binCV bar and lives
     in cuda_sparse_benchmark. SPEED VERDICT: OUTSTANDING.

 calcOpticalFlowBlockMatch
     Pyramidal tracking by integer Hamming block matching. The
     nearest cv::cuda call is SparsePyrLKOpticalFlow, which solves
     a different equation -- and it is already section 14's bar
     for the op that DOES solve the same one. Quoting it twice
     would make one denominator answer two questions. SPEED
     VERDICT: OUTSTANDING; the role row that exists is the
     sparse family's own, stated as a role comparison there.

 matchDescriptorsGated
     Section 13 prices the UNGATED matcher, which is the one with
     a counterpart. The gate changes the ADMITTED SET, and
     cv::cuda::DescriptorMatcher has no mask that reproduces it,
     so the gated form has no bar. SPEED VERDICT: OUTSTANDING.

 the RANSAC geometry stage
     NOT an outstanding row and must not be filed as one. OpenCV
     has no cv::cuda essential-matrix estimator, but it DOES ship
     a GPU RANSAC (cv::cuda::solvePnPRansac, cudalegacy) whose
     shape is the finding: it keeps the solver on the host and
     moves only scoring. binCV's own measurement came out the
     same way and the stage stays on the host. There is nothing
     here to ship and therefore nothing to leave outstanding.
OUTSTANDING,covariance
OUTSTANDING,cornerSubPixAsync
OUTSTANDING,gftt_device_spacing
OUTSTANDING,keypointsFromCorners
OUTSTANDING,keypointOrientation
OUTSTANDING,stereoDescriptorMatch
OUTSTANDING,stereoRefineDisparity
OUTSTANDING,stereoMatchRectified
OUTSTANDING,calcOpticalFlowBlockMatch
