 CUDA ROLE COMPARISONS -- every cv::cuda bar, one process, one protocol
=======================================================================
 device: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3070 Ti (sm_86, 48 SMs, 608 GB/s peak)
 OpenCV 4.5.4
 frames: 752x480 (the reference geometry, where every written bar was
 set) and 1920x1080 (carried because several of these ops sit ON the
 launch floor at the reference size, where a ratio between two arms is
 a ratio between two launches).

 ORIENTATION: every ratio below is binCV / OpenCV. Under 1.000x is
 binCV ahead. Arm A is OpenCV, arm B is binCV, in every pair.

 LAUNCH FLOOR (empty kernel, same protocol)      0.0058 ms  spread   34%  [kernel]
   Every kernel-resident number below sits on top of this. An arm within
   a few multiples of it is reporting launch overhead, not its own work,
   and a RATIO between two such arms is reporting almost none of either.
   The spread is part of the answer, not a blemish on it: under WSL2 a
   launch costs what it costs plus whatever the host was doing.
FLOOR,0.005274,0.005786,0.007250

 Driver meter step, measured in this run: 2.00 MB. Every memory
 reading below is taken over 256 working sets for that reason.

=====================================================================
 1. THRESHOLD -- binCV cuda::threshold vs cv::cuda::threshold
=====================================================================
 ROLE: 'turn a wide frame into a per-pixel above/below map on device'.
 Both sides are ONE launch. They differ in what they emit: OpenCV
 writes one BYTE per pixel (CV_8UC1, THRESH_BINARY), binCV writes one
 BIT. That difference is the whole point of the library and it is
 stated at the number rather than folded into it -- the outputs are
 not the same array, so this is a role comparison, not an equality.

 threshold, uint8 -> above/below map  [752x480]
 cv::cuda::threshold -> CV_8U                     0.008 ms  spread   35%  [kernel]  AT THE LAUNCH FLOOR
 bincv::cuda::threshold -> bits                   0.008 ms  spread  276%  [kernel]  AT THE LAUNCH FLOOR
