   cv::cuda::createMedianFilter's scratch is NOT incidental: its
   own filtering.cpp sizes the histograms at cols*256*partitions
   and cols*8*partitions CV_32S, which at 752 columns and its
   default 128 partitions is ~98 MB of device scratch for ONE
   752x480 frame. binCV's medianWide allocates ZERO scratch --
   no heap in kernels is a project rule, so the caller's two
   images are the whole working set.

 medianWide working set (wide src + wide dst + filter buffer)  [752x480]  -- METER 2 on BOTH sides (cudaMemGetInfo delta),
 each side over enough independent working sets that the meter's
 2.00 MB unit resolves, then divided by its own count.
   [meter 2] cv::cuda      792.00 MB over    8 sets =  101376.0 KB/frame  (396 units)
   [meter 2] binCV         206.00 MB over  256 sets =     824.0 KB/frame  (103 units)
   [meter 2] ROUNDING: one 2.00 MB unit is 8.0 KB/frame at 256 sets (0.97% of binCV's
             figure) and 256.0 KB/frame at 8 sets (0.25% of OpenCV's).
   ratio OpenCV/binCV = 123.029x  -- binCV is SMALLER by 123.029x.
   The OpenCV side is an UPPER BOUND: GpuMat pads its pitch, and any
   filter buffer or NPP scratch held past the call is inside this
   delta. binCV's side is cudaMalloc with nothing in between.
   NOTE: medianWide claims NO footprint advantage -- it is one
   byte per pixel on both sides. Any ratio here is GpuMat's pitch
   padding plus whatever cv::cuda::createMedianFilter holds for
   its histogram scratch, and it is NOT evidence for 1-bit
   packing. denoiseMedian3 is where this family's footprint
   result lives, and its bar is the binCV byte arm above.
