=====================================================================
 13. DESCRIPTOR MATCHING -- binCV cuda::matchDescriptors vs
     cv::cuda::DescriptorMatcher::createBFMatcher(NORM_HAMMING)
=====================================================================
 ROLE: 'nearest neighbour over binary descriptors with Lowe's ratio
 test, for a whole query set, on device'. A binCV device matcher
 EXISTS as of this round (cuda/sparseMatch.hpp), so the row this
 file used to print -- 'no binCV device counterpart' -- is retired.

 WHAT THE TIMED REGION IS, AND WHY IT FAVOURS OpenCV. binCV's arm
 is ONE launch that produces the ratio-tested result. OpenCV's arm
 is knnMatchAsync(k=2) ALONE -- the ratio test that would turn its
 two distances into the same answer is NOT in the bracket, and
 neither is knnMatchConvert. So the OpenCV arm is timed doing
 strictly LESS work than binCV's, and every ratio below is an
 UPPER BOUND on OpenCV's standing. That is deliberate: a bar the
 challenger cannot be accused of shaving is worth more than a bar
 that is exactly fair, when the challenger wins anyway.

 TWO OpenCV ARMS, because the bar is the BEST existing option.
 cv::cuda instantiates matchHamming_gpu at <unsigned char> and at
 <int>, and cv::cuda::ORB emits CV_8U. Both are run over the SAME
 descriptor bytes, reinterpreted, so the two arms differ only in
 the type the kernel was instantiated at.

 CONTENT. Pseudorandom descriptor words. Neither kernel has a
 data-dependent early-out -- both evaluate every (query, train)
 pair -- so the cost is content-independent and a real descriptor
 set would move neither arm. What content DOES decide is accuracy,
 which this row does not measure and does not claim.

