=====================================================================
 8. THE DEFAULT-STREAM SURCHARGE -- why every row above runs on an
    EXPLICIT stream, measured rather than asserted
=====================================================================
 OpenCV's CUDA modules call cudaDeviceSynchronize() after a launch
 WHEN AND ONLY WHEN the stream is the null stream. It is not one
 function: the guard

     if (stream == 0) cudaSafeCall( cudaDeviceSynchronize() );

 appears in cudev's grid transform (which backs cudaarithm's
 threshold, abs and max), in cudafilters' morphology, linear and
 median filters, in cudawarping's resize and pyrDown, and three
 times in cudastereo's StereoBM.

 CONSEQUENCE FOR A BENCHMARK. On the default stream an OpenCV arm
 cannot pipeline across a batch of enqueues while binCV's arm can,
 so an event bracket over N calls measures N serialized round trips
 on one side and N pipelined launches on the other. That is not a
 kernel-to-kernel comparison, and the difference is not small.

 WHICH NUMBER IS THE BAR. The explicit-stream one. CLAUDE.md says the
 bar is the BEST existing option, not the worst; a resident pipeline
 uses streams, and OpenCV supports them on every call measured here.
 Quoting the default-stream number would be measuring against a
 fallback nobody would use. The default-stream figure is reported
 beside it as what a naive caller pays, and labelled as that.

