=====================================================================
 3. MEDIAN (WIDE) -- binCV cuda::medianWide vs
    cv::cuda::createMedianFilter(CV_8UC1, ksize)
=====================================================================
 ROLE ONLY, and the asymmetry is stated first because it is large:
 cv::cuda::createMedianFilter is a 3x3 (or 5x5) SQUARE median with a
 replicated border. binCV's kMedianReferenceL is a 3-sample L and
 kMedianReferencePlus is a 5-sample plus, both with a ZERO border.
 These produce DIFFERENT IMAGES. Neither is an oracle for the other
 and no correctness claim is made across this row -- binCV's
 correctness is closed against its own host twin by the test suite.
 What this prices is the ROLE: 'median-filter a resident 8-bit frame
 on the device'. Part of binCV's advantage here is that its operation
 is CHEAPER (3 or 5 samples against 9), which is the algorithm's win
 and NOT the representation's -- medianWide has no packed advantage
 and its own header says so. The K=9 row below is the closest
 sample-count match to a 3x3 square and is the fairest single row.
 MEMORY IS PARITY BY CONSTRUCTION: one byte per pixel in, one out,
 on both sides. Any difference in the meter is GpuMat's pitch
 padding, not a representation win, and it is labelled as such.

